  "query_preview": "..Mostly sunny skies this afternoon across southeast \nArizona with just some high thin clouds traversing through the area \nfrom north to south on the eastern flank of ridging aloft. This \nridging will be in control of our weather through the weekend with \ndry conditions continuing along with above normal temperatures. The \nwarmest day will be Sunday with highs about 7 to 12 degrees above \nnormal. \n\nA more active weather pattern will take shape next week as the \nsouthern stream becomes more active. The weather will remain benign \nMonday as the area remains under westerly flow aloft with the \nridging over the weekend having flattened out. Temperatures will \nstill remain about 5 to 10 degrees above normal Monday. \n\nThe more noticeable changes will start on Tuesday ahead of a strong \ntrough/upper low moving into southern California from the eastern \nPacific. Ahead of this system, southwesterly flow will increase \nresulting in breezy to locally windy south to southwest winds around \n15 to 30 mph with gusts up to 45 mph. This could result in some \nareas of blowing dust Tuesday afternoon. Most of the daytime Tuesday \nshould remain dry, though a few showers could start sneaking in \nmainly west of Tucson later in the day. Temperatures will still be \nabout 3 to 8 degrees above normal.\n\nEnsembles are in really good agreement on the upper low as it \ntraverses across southern California Tuesday night and Arizona on \nWednesday. Just seeing some very minor differences in the timing and \namplitude but the consistency is quite impressive this far out. As \nthis system moves through Tuesday night through Wednesday and even \npotentially lingering into Thursday morning north and east of \nTucson, we'll see a period of strong lift/dynamics and moisture \nconvergence with moderate to strong IVT magnitudes around 750. This \nwill result in a period of widespread valley rain along with some \nvery high elevation snow late Tuesday night through Wednesday. \nLatest indications on the Atmospheric River scale is that this will \nbe an AR1 to AR2 level event, with the main limiting factor being \nthe residence time around 24 hours. \n\nPotential rain amounts have been trending upwards in the past day or \nso and now are in the 0.75\" to 1.5\" range for valleys with locally \nhigher amounts and 1 to 2\"+ of rain or liquid equivalent in the \nmountains. As the heaviest period of rain moves through late Tuesday \nnight or Wednesday, rain rates may be high enough to result in \nflowing washes and/or urban and small stream flooding. A rumble or \ntwo of thunder can't be ruled out as well. Given the deepest plume \nof moisture driving the bulk of the precipitation is of subtropical \norigin that will keep the snow levels pretty high for the duration \nof the event. Early indications are snow levels around 8500 feet \nTuesday night dropping to around 7500 to 8000 feet Wednesday \nafternoon into Wednesday night. As a result, snow amounts will be \nlimited to around 1 to 3 inches above 8000 feet in the Catalina \nMountains, 2 to 5 inches above 7500 feet for the Pinaleno \nMountains/Mount Graham and 4 to 9 inches above 7500 feet for the \nWhite Mountains of northern Greenlee County. Otherwise, much cooler \ntemperatures Wednesday with highs around 4 to 8 degrees below \nnormal. \n\nAny lingering showers north and east of Tucson are expected to end \nThursday morning as shortwave ridging builds in Thursday afternoon \nand continuing Friday with temperatures near to slightly below \nnormal. Thereafter, another potential system next weekend as an \nactive weather pattern continues.",
